! Vocabulary standard

<div class="mandarin-hero"><div class="mandarin-hero-label">Curriculum target</div><div class="mandarin-hero-zh">七千基础词 · 武侠专项</div><div class="mandarin-pinyin">qī qiān jīchǔ cí · wǔxiá zhuānxiàng</div><div>7,000 general Mandarin words plus 1,000–2,000 additional wuxia/xianxia words.</div></div>

!! General Mandarin: 7,000 entries

|!Band|!Target|!Selection principle|
|Fundamental|2,000|Core high-frequency spoken + written vocabulary, including grammar words|
|High-use|3,000|Frequent broader conversation, media, social, written and narrative vocabulary|
|High-availability|2,000|Words commonly *known* but not necessarily frequently said or written: practical familiar things and concepts|

!! Wuxia/xianxia specialization: +1,000–2,000

Genre vocabulary for martial hierarchy, swords and weapons, techniques, inner power/cultivation, historical and literary discourse, kinship, social conventions and narrative formulas. Where a word is already in the core 7k, reuse its entry and tag it for wuxia; ''do not count it twice.''

!! Coverage goal

Aim for ''95–98% lexical token coverage'' of designated material, with spoken conversation, general written text and wuxia literature reported ''separately''. This is a ''measured objective, not a promise'' that 7,000 words cover all genres.

The Italian ''Vocabolario di base'' framework inspired these three bands, but its frequency percentages cannot be transplanted directly to Mandarin. In one published Taiwan Mandarin corpus comparison, 7,000 rank-ordered words covered 96.95% of spontaneous conversation, but only 82.55% of written text. Different registers demand different learning plans.

!! Sourcing approach

Use a ''curated combination'' of SUBTLEX-CH (spoken), wordfreq zh (mixed registers), the November 2025 11,000-entry HSK exam syllabus (pedagogical alignment, *not frequency*), and a deliberately constructed high-availability and genre-specific supplement. Never silently treat character frequency as word frequency.

